// core/automation - the foundational "automation layer" contract.
//
// Term and design defined directly by the user this session: a host
// embedding Frust (a game engine, a DAW, an IDE plugin system - anything)
// needs to load *some* stateful thing that turns a per-tick delta-time
// into a value, without knowing ahead of time which concrete kind it is.
// A game engine moving a sphere up and down and a DAW automating a filter
// cutoff are the same shape from Frust's side: something with real
// internal state (a phase, a rate, whatever) that the host calls once a
// frame with `tick(delta_time)`, and applies the returned value to
// whatever it's actually driving. Frust never needs to know what a
// "sphere" or a "filter" is - that's entirely the host's business.
//
// This is genuine interface dispatch, not convention - `Automation` is a
// real `interface`, and `impl Automation for X` is checked at compile
// time (every declared method must actually be implemented, or codegen
// rejects it). A host can hold an `Automation`-typed value without
// knowing or caring which concrete struct is behind it, and calling
// `.tick()` on it dispatches through a real vtable to whichever concrete
// implementation is actually there.
//
// Deliberately foundational and host-agnostic, not audio/JUCE-specific -
// domain-specific automations (audio parameters, engine transforms) are a
// separate, later concern built on top of this, not part of this file.

interface Automation {
    fn tick(&mut self, delta_time: f32) -> f32
}

// A linear ramp: phase accumulates at a fixed rate every tick, forever.
// The simplest possible real Automation - useful on its own (a steadily
// rising/falling control value) and as the reference case for verifying
// any new Automation dispatches correctly.
struct RampAutomation {
    phase: f32,
    rate: f32
}

impl Automation for RampAutomation {
    pub fn tick(&mut self, delta_time: f32) -> f32 = {
        self.phase = self.phase + delta_time * self.rate;
        self.phase
    }
}

// Exponential decay toward zero: each tick multiplies the current value
// by a fixed factor. Deliberately a structurally different update rule
// than RampAutomation (accumulate-and-add vs. multiply-in-place) - the
// two together are what actually prove dynamic dispatch works, not just
// that one Automation compiles.
struct DecayAutomation {
    value: f32,
    factor: f32
}

impl Automation for DecayAutomation {
    pub fn tick(&mut self, delta_time: f32) -> f32 = {
        self.value = self.value * self.factor;
        self.value
    }
}

// Verified (frust_compiler, Debug): a RampAutomation{phase:0.0, rate:2.0}
// stored in an Automation-typed `let` and ticked three times with
// delta_time=1.0 produced 2, 4, 6 - a DecayAutomation{value:100.0,
// factor:0.5} through the SAME call-site pattern produced 50, 25, 12.5.
// Same `.tick()` call site, two different concrete types, correct
// per-instance state maintained across calls for both - genuine
// polymorphic dispatch, not two independent special cases.

// Host-callable constructors, for a native host (frust_plugin_host,
// e.g. the JUCE IDE's AutomationPanel) that wants to build one of these
// and hold onto it across many later calls - not just Frust code calling
// Frust code in the same stack frame. `own` heap-allocates (see
// Codegen.h's compileHeapStructLiteral), so the returned pointer stays
// valid after this function returns - a plain struct literal would NOT
// survive that (it's stack-allocated in THIS function's own frame).
//
// A host calls these via frust_plugin_get_fn(handle, "new_ramp") cast to
// (void*(*)(float))(...), then ticks the result via
// frust_plugin_get_fn(handle, "RampAutomation::tick") cast to
// (float(*)(void*, float))(...) - the mangled method name IS the real
// exported symbol, same as any other impl method. The host never needs
// to know about the Automation interface or its vtable at all; it's
// calling the concrete type's own method directly.
pub fn new_ramp(rate: f32) -> RampAutomation = {
    own RampAutomation { phase: 0.0, rate: rate }
}
pub fn new_decay(factor: f32) -> DecayAutomation = {
    own DecayAutomation { value: 100.0, factor: factor }
}
